@page "/"
@inject Lobby Lobby
@inject NavigationManager Navigation

@*
    The lobby: what is open, who is still waiting, and how to open another one.

    ⚠️ Static SSR, by default and deliberately (§3.11 C12). The table is the one interactive
    island in this client; a list of tables and two forms is a page, and a page whose every
    control is a real one — a <form>, a <button>, an <a> — needs no circuit to work at all
    (§3.11 A4). Sitting down is a GET, because it navigates; opening a table is a POST,
    because it makes one.

    🔥 Your name is typed here rather than configured, because a lobby is where a table learns
    who is coming. It travels in the URL. ⚠️ A name is not a credential and this page does not
    pretend it is one: whoever types a name that is already at a table takes that seat back,
    which is exactly what makes a browser refresh put you back in your own chair (§3.11 C16).
*@

<PageTitle>Burmese Poker — the tables</PageTitle>

<section class="lobby">
    <h1>The tables</h1>

    @if (_problem is { } wrong)
    {
        <p class="problem" role="alert">@wrong</p>
    }

    <ul class="tables">
        @foreach (var table in _tables)
        {
            <li @key="table.Id" class="open">
                <h2>@table.Title</h2>

                <p class="shape">
                    <span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">🪑</span>
                    <span>@Shape(table)</span>
                </p>

                <p class="shape">
                    <span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">🤖</span>
                    <span>@Opponents(table)</span>
                </p>

                <p class="state">@State(table)</p>

                @*
                    🔥 Shown even when every seat is held, and that is not an oversight. Blazor
                    keeps a dropped circuit alive for minutes before disposing it, so somebody
                    who reloaded finds their own seat still occupied — by their own ghost — and
                    a form that hid itself would lock them out of the table they are sitting at.
                    Sitting down under a name already here takes that seat back (§3.11 C16).
                *@
                <form class="sit" method="get" action="@($"/table/{table.Id}")">
                    <label for="@($"you-{table.Id}")">Your name</label>
                    <input id="@($"you-{table.Id}")" name="you" type="text"
                           maxlength="24" value="@Lobby.You" autocomplete="nickname" />
                    <button type="submit">Sit down</button>
                </form>

                <p class="watch">
                    <a href="@($"/table/{table.Id}")">Watch this table</a>
                </p>

                @*
                    🔥 Open a table, then send somebody the link — which is the whole of how a
                    friend joins this site (BUILD-PLAN P54, HOSTING.md §6 Step 4).

                    ⚠️ The link itself is an <a>, and the copy button is an enhancement on top of
                    it (§3.11 C12, A4). A page whose every control is a real one works with no
                    circuit and no script at all, and copying text is the one thing on it that a
                    browser cannot do without JavaScript — so the address is written out in full
                    and can be selected, shared or long-pressed whether or not the script ran.
                    The button is hidden until the script says otherwise, and it says so by
                    marking the document rather than the button: enhanced navigation swaps this
                    markup out from under any per-element work done at load.

                    ⚠️ Absolute, out of NavigationManager, so it is the address a phone can
                    actually reach. Behind Traefik that is the forwarded host — which is P51's
                    UseForwardedHeaders doing the one job nothing else can do for it.
                *@
                <p class="link">
                    <a href="@Link(table)" class="url">@Link(table)</a>
                    <button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="@Link(table)">Copy link</button>
                </p>
            </li>
        }
    </ul>

    <details class="opening">
        <summary>Open another table</summary>

        @*
            🔥 No <AntiforgeryToken /> here, and that is the fix rather than the omission.
            EditForm renders one itself for a static SSR post; adding a second put two inputs
            of the same name in the form, the two arrived as one comma-joined value, and the
            post was rejected — with a page that had rendered perfectly. Found by pressing the
            button, which is the only thing that was ever going to find it (P13.3's rule: ask
            the server for what the page says it will do).
        *@
        <EditForm FormName="open" Model="Wanted!" OnValidSubmit="Open" method="post">
            <p>
                <label for="new-title">Called</label>
                <InputText id="new-title" @bind-Value="Wanted!.Title" maxlength="40" />
            </p>

            @*
                🔥 Five, not four (BUILD-PLAN P56 item 4). The default is RoundEngine's
                DefaultPlayers, which is the table this game is played at and the table every
                published measurement is made at — MinimumPlayers is merely the smallest legal
                one, and reading the floor as the default is exactly the accident P32 found one
                layer down.
            *@
            <p>
                <label for="new-seats">Seats</label>
                <InputNumber id="new-seats" @bind-Value="Wanted!.Seats"
                             min="@RoundEngine.MinimumPlayers" max="@RoundEngine.MaximumPlayers" />
            </p>

            @*
                🔥 People is a quorum, and this is the one thing on the form a person cannot
                guess (BUILD-PLAN P56 item 1). A table deals when EVERY seat held for a person
                has somebody in it, so a table opened for four friends sits there doing nothing
                until the fourth arrives — which is indistinguishable from a broken site unless
                the form says so before you press the button and the list says so afterwards.
            *@
            <p>
                <label for="new-people">Of those, people</label>
                <InputNumber id="new-people" @bind-Value="Wanted!.People" min="0" max="@RoundEngine.MaximumPlayers" />
            </p>

            <p class="note" id="new-people-note">
                It waits for all of them: the first card is dealt when every seat you keep for a
                person has somebody in it. Ask for one and you can sit down and play on your
                own; ask for four and nothing happens until your fourth friend arrives; ask for
                none and it is a table to watch rather than to join.
            </p>

            @*
                🔥 The difficulty dial, out of DifficultyLadder, strongest first (P19). P18 put
                the skill ladder here — which is a research instrument wearing a menu's clothes,
                because its rungs are far apart, cannot be asked for "a bit easier", and play a
                different and worse idea rather than the right idea badly. These are levels: the
                strongest rung there is, with a measured mistake rate (§3.12).

                🔥 And behind them, since P56, the ladder itself — every rung, each with the
                margin that prices it. That is an amendment to §3.12 rather than a lapse from
                it: the thing it forbade was selling a measured-worse opponent as a matter of
                taste, and a rung offered WITH its measured margin is not being sold as taste.
                Levels are the menu; rungs are an advanced disclosure that states its price.

                ⚠️ The prices come out of docs/strategy/measurements.csv through OpponentMenu
                and are fenced by PublishedFigureTests. A rung with no published row is not
                offered at all.
            *@
            <p>
                <label for="new-difficulty">The computer plays</label>
                <InputSelect id="new-difficulty" @bind-Value="Wanted!.Difficulty">
                    @OpponentOptions
                </InputSelect>
            </p>

            @*
                🔥 Difficulty is per seat, and this is how a person asks for that (P19, P56). A
                table of four identical bots is the least interesting table in the game.

                ⚠️ Three answers rather than P19's checkbox, and the third takes a second post:
                a static SSR page cannot grow a control per seat as the seat count is typed
                (§3.11 C12), so the shape is settled first and the seats are chosen on the page
                that comes back. Every control on this page stays a real one.
            *@
            <p>
                <label for="new-fill">The computer's seats</label>
                <InputSelect id="new-fill" @bind-Value="Wanted!.Fill">
                    @foreach (var (value, label) in SeatFill.Offered)
                    {
                        <option @key="value" value="@value">@label</option>
                    }
                </InputSelect>
            </p>

            @if (_choosing && Wanted!.PerSeat.Count > 0)
            {
                <fieldset class="seats">
                    <legend>Each computer seat</legend>

                    <p class="note">
                        In seating order, after the seats kept for people. Press
                        <em>Open it</em> again when they read the way you want them to.
                    </p>

                    @for (var index = 0; index < Wanted!.PerSeat.Count; index++)
                    {
                        var seat = index;

                        <p>
                            <label for="@($"new-seat-{seat}")">Seat @(Wanted!.WantedPeople + seat + 1)</label>
                            <InputSelect id="@($"new-seat-{seat}")" @bind-Value="Wanted!.PerSeat[seat]">
                                @OpponentOptions
                            </InputSelect>
                        </p>
                    }
                </fieldset>
            }

            @*
                🔥 How long the seating holds (RULES.md §3 step 2, P36). Held is the rule and the
                default: the seats are drawn when the table opens and kept, so the ring stops
                rearranging itself around you every deal.

                ⚠️ This is a house arrangement and NOT the rule about changing seats. §3 says the
                seats change when the players AGREE, and a number chosen before anybody sits down
                is not people agreeing — that is packet P37, and this control is the thing it
                replaces rather than a first version of it.
            *@
            <p>
                <label for="new-seating">The seats are</label>
                <InputSelect id="new-seating" @bind-Value="Wanted!.Seating">
                    @foreach (var policy in SeatingPolicy.Offered)
                    {
                        <option @key="policy.Name" value="@policy.Name">@policy.Name — @policy.Description</option>
                    }
                </InputSelect>
            </p>

            <p class="note">
                The rest are played by the computer, and each is named for how it plays. A table
                with no people at it still deals, and is worth watching. A seat the computer
                takes over for somebody who has stopped answering plays as well as it can,
                whatever this says.
            </p>

            <p>
                <button type="submit">Open it</button>
            </p>
        </EditForm>
    </details>
</section>

@code {
    private IReadOnlyList<HostedTable> _tables = [];
    private string? _problem;

    /// <summary>Whether this render is the second step of an open: the seats, one by one.</summary>
    private bool _choosing;

    /// <remarks>
    /// ⚠️ <b>Bound from the form, so it survives the post</b> — a static SSR page is built again
    /// from scratch on the way back, and a model held only in a field would come back empty.
    /// </remarks>
    [SupplyParameterFromForm(FormName = "open")]
    private NewTable? Wanted { get; set; }

    /// <remarks>
    /// ⚠️ <b>The model is filled in here and never by an initialiser</b> (BL0008): a form post
    /// binds this property, and a property with an initialiser can be overwritten with null on
    /// the way through.
    /// </remarks>
    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        Wanted ??= new NewTable
        {
            Seats = Lobby.Opening.Seats,
            People = Lobby.Opening.People,
            Difficulty = Lobby.Opening.Difficulty,
            Fill = Lobby.Opening.Difficulties is { Count: > 0 } ? SeatFill.Mixed : SeatFill.Same,
            Seating = Lobby.Opening.Seating
        };

        _tables = Lobby.Tables;
    }

    /// <remarks>
    /// 🔥 <b>One button and two steps</b> (BUILD-PLAN P56). Asking for a table seat by seat
    /// needs a control per computer seat, and a static SSR page cannot grow one as the seat
    /// count is typed (§3.11 C12) — so a post that has settled the shape but not the seats
    /// renders the seats instead of opening the table, and the next press opens it. ⚠️ <b>It is
    /// the model that decides which post this is</b>, by counting: change the shape on the
    /// second step and the seats are asked again, because the answers no longer fit the table.
    /// </remarks>
    private void Open()
    {
        var wanted = Wanted ?? new NewTable();

        if (wanted.NeedsSeatChoices)
        {
            wanted.AskForSeats();
            _choosing = true;
            _tables = Lobby.Tables;
            return;
        }

        try
        {
            var table = Lobby.Open(wanted.Wanted(Lobby.Opening, Lobby.NextSeed()));

            Navigation.NavigateTo($"/table/{table.Id}");
        }
        catch (InvalidOperationException full)
        {
            _problem = full.Message;
            _tables = Lobby.Tables;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>This table's address, in full, as a person would paste it to somebody.</summary>
    private string Link(HostedTable table) =>
        Navigation.ToAbsoluteUri($"table/{table.Id}").ToString();

    private static string Shape(HostedTable table) =>
        $"{table.Seats} seats, {table.Plan.People} for people";

    /// <summary>
    /// How hard the computer is here — one word, or the mix itself.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// ⚠️ <b>A mixed table has no one answer</b> (P19), so it is not given one: the levels are
    /// listed in the order the seats are dealt them, which is what the seat names on the table
    /// itself will say.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string Opponents(HostedTable table) => table.Difficulties switch
    {
        { Count: 0 } => "Every seat is for a person",
        { } levels when table.IsMixed =>
            "The computer plays " + string.Join(", ", levels.Select(OpponentMenu.Called)),
        { } levels => $"The computer plays {OpponentMenu.Called(levels[0])}"
    };

    /// <remarks>
    /// <b>What the table is doing, and why.</b> A table waits for the seats it was opened with —
    /// P13.4's finding, that an unanswered seat spends its whole patience on every question, is
    /// only fixable by something that knows whether anybody is there (BUILD-PLAN P13.6).
    /// </remarks>
    /// <remarks>
    /// 🔥 <b>A table waiting for people is not a broken one, and the sentence has to say so</b>
    /// (BUILD-PLAN P56). <c>TablePlan.People</c> is a quorum — <c>HostedTable.Ready</c> wants
    /// every person-seat claimed — so a table opened for five friends deals nothing at all
    /// until the fifth arrives, and without a sentence saying which it is waiting for, a table
    /// that is working perfectly reads as a table that is stuck.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string State(HostedTable table) => table switch
    {
        { WaitingFor.Count: > 0 } waiting =>
            $"Waiting for {Count(waiting.WaitingFor.Count)} to sit down; it deals when they are all here.",
        { IsDealing: true } dealing when dealing.Board.Round > 0 =>
            $"Playing round {dealing.Board.Round}. Every seat is taken; your own name takes yours back.",
        { IsDealing: true } => "Dealing.",
        _ => "Nobody here. It deals again when somebody arrives."
    };

    /// <summary>"one more person", "three more people" — the sentence, not the number.</summary>
    private static string Count(int people) => people == 1 ? "one more person" : $"{people} more people";

    /// <summary>
    /// Every opponent this lobby offers: the four levels, and the ladder behind an advanced
    /// group with each rung's measured price beside it.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// ⚠️ <b>One list, used by the whole-table control and by every per-seat one</b>, because a
    /// per-seat picker whose options are levels-plus-rungs is one design and not two — the
    /// mistake it avoids is designing the same menu twice and letting the two drift.
    /// </remarks>
    private RenderFragment OpponentOptions => @<text>
        @foreach (var level in OpponentMenu.Levels)
        {
            <option @key="level.Name" value="@level.Name">@level.Name — @level.Description</option>
        }

        <optgroup label="Advanced — the measured ladder, and what each rung costs">
            @foreach (var opponent in OpponentMenu.Advanced)
            {
                <option @key="opponent.Value" value="@opponent.Value">
                    @opponent.Rung.Name — @opponent.Rung.Description — @opponent.Price
                </option>
            }
        </optgroup>
    </text>;
}
